course: dictionary
lesson: 8687
title: Big words with clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Segregation is a big word, but the words around it give clues. «Rosa Parks refused to give up her bus seat, and the bus boycott that followed helped end segregation laws, which kept Black and white people apart on buses, in schools and in shops.» The words kept apart tell you that segregation means separating people.
explain: Segregation has two meanings. The first is about people: keeping groups apart by law or custom because of their race, religion or sex. This is unfair and wrong, and people fought against it. The second is more formal and is about things: separating them into groups, such as «the segregation of waste for recycling».
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. «New laws in the 1960s banned segregation» still makes sense if you think "banned keeping groups apart by law". Ask yourself: are people being kept apart, or are things being sorted? The clues will tell you which meaning you need.
fact: Segregation means keeping groups apart, or separating things into groups.
fact: For people, segregation means keeping groups apart by law or custom because of race, religion or sex. It is unfair and wrong.
fact: In the USA, segregation laws kept Black and white people apart on buses, in schools and in shops.
fact: In 1955 Rosa Parks refused to give up her bus seat in Montgomery, Alabama. The bus boycott that followed helped end these laws.
fact: For things, segregation means sorting them into groups, as in «the segregation of waste for recycling».
exercise: «New laws in the 1960s banned segregation, keeping Black and white people apart by law.» What does segregation mean here? | Sorting rubbish for recycling | *Keeping groups apart by law because of their race | Joining groups together as friends
exercise: «The factory staff began the segregation of waste for recycling.» What does segregation mean here? | Throwing rubbish away | Keeping people apart by law | Running a bus boycott | *Sorting things into groups
exercise: Rosa Parks broke a segregation law in 1955. What did such laws do? | Made buses run faster | Gave everyone free bus tickets | Closed all the schools | *Kept Black and white people apart on buses
test: The text says segregation laws «kept Black and white people apart». What is the best meaning of segregation? | *Keeping groups apart by law or custom because of race | Working together in the same school | Sorting rubbish | Being friends with everyone
test: Ruby reads «the segregation of waste for recycling». What does the word tell her? | People are being kept apart by law | A boycott is happening | *Things are being separated into groups | Waste is being thrown away
exam: Mia says segregation is always about people. Is she right? | Yes, because it always means a bus boycott | *No, it can also mean separating things into groups, such as waste for recycling | No, it means joining groups together | Yes, it is only ever about people
